Description: Enigma2 IPTV player with TMDB movie discovery and VOD downloads
 R123 derives web asset versions from the installed package metadata instead
 of a stale fixed web revision. Matching web and running receiver versions
 no longer show a false restart warning; real update mismatches and missing
 API warnings remain visible. R122 independent subtitle keys are preserved.
 R122 makes the first API key row follow the selected subtitle provider.
 All key labels, masked values, keyboard entry and connection tests resolve
 the same provider, while SubDL, OpenSubtitles.com and SubSource credentials
 remain independently saved and preserved when switching providers.
 R121 adds SubSource to the receiver's combined subtitle search, results and
 provider filter. Its API key is shared with the web subtitle settings; no
 username or password is required. IMDb/title lookup, primary/secondary
 languages, bounded downloads and episode selection from season ZIPs use
 the official SubSource API. The R120 translations and artwork are retained.
 R120 completes the independent subtitle settings translations in all 43
 packaged receiver language catalogs, including row labels, appearance values,
 live preview, API status, provider connection feedback and the yellow API test
 remote-control caption. Device-language selection and the R119 subtitle
 synchronization, layouts, images, controls and saved settings are retained.
 R119 uses decoder-authoritative independent subtitle timing: stalled or
 invalid PTS can no longer start an unbounded substitute clock. Seek requests
 suspend captions until actual decoder timing settles; success, cancellation,
 timeout and service changes clear pending timing state. Sub-second position
 precision, explicit pause/resume, remux offsets and the existing UHD/FHD/HD/SD
 layouts are retained. Provider FPS alone no longer stretches SRT timestamps;
 different frame rates remain matching hints. Bounded diagnostic samples
 distinguish decoder, stalled, missing and seek timing without stream secrets.
 R117 redesigns Independent Subtitle Settings as the approved two-panel glass
 page: all SubDL/OpenSubtitles credentials and search/appearance controls stay
 available on the left, while live preview, API state and connection feedback
 remain visible on the right. Row arrows, saved focus colours and the shared
 translated remote guide scale at UHD, FHD, HD and 4:3 SD GUI resolutions.
 R116 redesigns the combined subtitle browser with translucent glass panels,
 framed compatibility badges and the plugin's shared remote-control guide.
 Its six translated actions and seven-row result layout scale independently
 for native UHD, FHD, HD and 4:3 SD Enigma2 GUI desktops while the opaque
 full-screen guard continues to prevent DVB video from leaking around the UI.
 R115 scores combined subtitle results by exact programme/episode identity,
 release source, edition, decoder FPS, timeline duration and codec/resolution.
 Five colour-coded compatibility classes are shown in all 43 device languages;
 safe film/PAL FPS pairs automatically retime cues without touching unknown or
 incompatible rates, and doubled 50/60 FPS decoder values are normalized.
 R114 adds a blue Edit action to the combined subtitle browser. The receiver
 keyboard can replace a bad portal movie/series name before the next combined
 SubDL and OpenSubtitles search; stale catalogue IDs and year are excluded from
 that manual lookup while episode and release-compatibility hints are retained.
 R113 normalizes decorated Stalker/MAC movie and series names before subtitle
 lookup, recovers release years embedded beside quality tags, and carries the
 portal detail title/year/TMDb/IMDb identity into player subtitle searches.
 Exact provider identity checks remain active, but a correct result is no
 longer rejected only because the portal appended language or release labels.
 R112 suspends the compact high-z independent-subtitle window while the opaque
 GT subtitle browser is open, preventing its transparent video rectangle from
 covering result rows. Closing the browser restores the current cue without
 losing timing, changing a user pause, or allowing stale-service callbacks.
 R111 adds one GT IPTV PLAYER PRO subtitle browser to the yellow-key menu. Its
 green key searches SubDL and OpenSubtitles together, the yellow key cycles an
 in-memory All/OpenSubtitles/SubDL filter, and every result has a local flag.
 The opaque interface scales across UHD, FHD, HD and SD GUI desktops and uses
 the existing translated UI vocabulary available in all 43 language catalogs.
 R110 removes bounded IPTV suffixes such as |DE and |4K before provider lookup,
 accepts an exact-title adjacent release year, retries OpenSubtitles once without
 its year filter, and logs bounded rejected-candidate identities for diagnosis.
 R109 retries empty or rejected SubDL/OpenSubtitles identifier searches with
 the cleaned title and year, verifies returned catalogue identity, and records
 credential-free search stages and result counts in the subtitle diagnostics.
 R108 verifies OpenSubtitles results against returned IMDb/TMDb identity or an
 exact normalized title/year, limits the provider list to 15 relevant entries,
 and anchors independent subtitle timing to the requested position after seek.
 R107 makes OpenSubtitles username and password optional: the API key alone
 enables connection tests and searches, while downloads use account login only
 when both optional fields are present and otherwise try the API-key route.
 R106 lists SubDL and OpenSubtitles.com together on every manual subtitle
 search, keeps both API-key rows visible, and recognizes legacy OpenSubtitles
 credential names without changing the provider used by automatic search.
 R105 adds OpenSubtitles.com to the independent subtitle workflow with
 provider selection, authenticated downloads and separate protected settings.
 R104 preserves millisecond decoder timing for independent subtitles and ranks
 same-language SubDL results by playback FPS, release type and resolution.
 R103 moves active independent subtitles above the player infobar while it is
 visible and narrows the subtitle window so neither OSD obscures the other.
 R102 replaces the full-screen transparent subtitle dialog with a compact,
 persistent high-z OSD window for Vu+/OpenPLi ServiceApp framebuffer drivers.
 R101 uses a pause-aware monotonic fallback when ServiceApp exposes an invalid
 or frozen decoder clock, and records bounded subtitle timing diagnostics.
 R100 raises the independent subtitle window above ServiceApp/ExtEplayer3's
 video plane so successfully loaded cues remain visible during playback.
 R99 honours the configured primary subtitle language before using the
 secondary language during automatic SubDL selection.
 R98 cleans IPTV provider/language suffixes and duplicate years before SubDL
 lookup, and treats an ordinary empty SubDL reply as no matching subtitles.
 R97 fixes independent subtitle rendering on playback engines that expose
 decoder time while reporting the stream as non-seekable.
