 R20 adds a responsive local web dashboard for phones, tablets and desktops.
 A one-time six-digit code plus explicit TV approval gates each browser;
 sessions are LAN-only, IP/device-bound, CSRF-protected and expire in memory.
 OpenWebif serves /gtiptv when available, with temporary port 9999 fallback.
 Source credentials and private keys are never returned to the browser.
 R21 replaces the native Web Interface footer sentence with the shared neon
 remote-control panel, coloured key badges and vertical dividers.
 R22 removes the inactive source-status card from the web dashboard and adds
 a dedicated automatic-test page for Xtream Codes and Stalker/MAC portals.
 Tests run sequentially in a cancellable background job and report protected
 traffic-light results, expiry, latency and connection use without exposing
 account credentials to the browser.
 R23 prevents mixed-version web bundles by disabling static-file caching and
 versioning browser assets. It also blocks external browser translation from
 corrupting interface labels and clearly requests an Enigma2 GUI restart when
 the installed web bundle and running receiver process do not match.
 R24 connects the built-in Xtream and Stalker TXT files on first use even
 when they are empty, including upgrades from R23 with no selected TXT.
 Explicit user-selected TXT files remain active.
 R25 saves preferred movie/series audio and subtitle languages and selects
 matching decoder tracks when they appear. Adds an authenticated responsive
 web remote and sends pasted text to an open receiver virtual keyboard.
 R26 waits for VOD playback to advance before selecting a preferred audio
 track, checks that the decoder accepted the selection, and recognizes
 Turkish track names when provider language metadata is missing. Streams
 without a working seek position retain their existing audio to avoid an
 unsafe startup track switch. Existing language catalogs are retained.
 R27 prevents unstable or sped-up VOD video on some ServiceApp receivers by
 suspending automatic audio-track switching during streamed playback. The
 saved audio preference is retained, and manual BLUE-key audio selection
 and preferred subtitles remain available. No UI translations change.
 R28 uses an optional bounded ffprobe lookup for movie and series audio
 languages on service type 5002. When a matching audio-track ordinal can be
 identified, ServiceApp passes it to exteplayer3 at startup. Missing ffprobe,
 unrecognized manifests and failed lookups keep the R27 playback path. All
 existing device-language catalogs remain and 42 audio-language choices are
 available in Settings; no running-video audio switch is scheduled.
 R29 removes preferred audio and subtitle languages from Settings and stops
 all automatic startup audio probing and playback subtitle selection. Saved
 preferences from earlier builds are ignored. Manual BLUE-key audio and
 YELLOW-key subtitle menus remain available; the 43 catalogs are retained.
 R30 puts manual audio and subtitle selection under YELLOW for movies and
 series; BLUE no longer opens VOD audio. The 1 key cycles installed playback
 engines for live TV, movies and episodes; INFO/EPG shows player information.
 Long LEFT/RIGHT opens an overlaid minute-entry seek dialog in movies and
 series while short presses retain their previous seek action. Engine changes
 preserve the same VOD URL and restore the episode position where possible.
 R31 binds YELLOW, 1 and VOD LEFT/RIGHT to remote events only while the player
 has focus, avoiding private keymap differences across Enigma2 images. Short
 LEFT/RIGHT retains each image's original action. Configured ServiceApp engines
 remain selectable when their executable is outside the usual search paths.
 R32 opens the minute seek dialog after a held VOD LEFT/RIGHT key even when an
 image supplies no long/repeat event. The dialog opens before decoder duration
 is ready; seeking is checked only after OK. A native number-input fallback
 opens if an image rejects the compact dialog.
 R33 raises the minute entry window above the external player's video and
 information layers and gives its input panel a readable background.
 R34 records bounded, credential-free diagnostic events for movie and series
 arrow holds and minute-dialog failures to identify receiver-specific errors.
 R35 uses the receiver's native number-entry dialog for minute seeking during
 movie and series playback, replacing an invisible custom modal on some images.
 The compact custom window remains an opaque fallback; playback stays active.
 R36 preallocates the native numeric input's four fixed-width digit slots so
 successive remote digits append to the minute value rather than overwrite it.
 R37 preserves explicitly supplied Stalker PHP endpoints, probes additional
 /c/ loader paths and validates live access before reporting portal health.
 A verified alternate MAG session can serve ordinary content requests after
 a bounded compatibility failure; explicit account denials remain terminal.
 R38 records credential-redacted Stalker request status, response shape,
 link choice and timed playback milestones for black-screen diagnostics.
 A country restriction is identified only from an explicit server response;
 otherwise the diagnosis remains unknown. No extra stream requests or
 automatic player restarts are introduced.
