 R40 adds web subtitle search for currently playing movies and episodes.
 SubDL, SubSource and OpenSubtitles use optional per-user API keys stored
 on the receiver; Subtitle Search Engine also offers keyless guest access.
 Results are matched by episode, selected through opaque short-lived IDs,
 downloaded with bounded HTTPS requests and loaded via SubsSupport/Pro.
 R41 allows alignment of a spoken movie timestamp with a downloaded SRT cue,
 plus half-second timing adjustments and reset from the paired web interface.
 The original SRT remains intact; the active movie is checked before loading.
 R42 shows decoder-provided movie and episode audio tracks in the paired web
 interface, with manual selection bound to the current item and track list.
 An absent dubbed language cannot be synthesized from an existing track.
 R43 adds mobile IPTV movie and series search with TV playback, web TMDb
 key setup, and a YouTube search/receiver playback screen with video quality
 preferences. Video stream extraction runs on the receiver without a
 separate YouTube plugin.
 R44 removes the YouTube Data API key requirement and searches the public
 YouTube results page from the receiver. Video quality controls remain;
 search and stream extraction may need updates when YouTube changes.
 R45 adds numbered, mobile-friendly YouTube search pages. A short-lived
 receiver-side continuation cursor fetches more public results without
 requiring a user API key; previously loaded pages stay available.
 R46 plays Discover Movies trailers through the bundled receiver-side
 YouTube resolver. Web and discovery extraction share one synchronization
 lock and the configured quality; no separate YouTube plugin is needed.
 R47 keeps fetching YouTube search pages while the search results provide a
 continuation. It ignores unrelated sidebar commands, accepts both response
 layouts, retains cursors for 30 minutes and exposes the next page number.
 R48 replaces the stream inside the existing web YouTube player when another
 video is sent to TV. This prevents modal player stacking, so one EXIT returns
 to the underlying plugin instead of revisiting previously played videos.
 R49 replaces Filter Search with MENU Hide All in the native Live TV, Movies
 and Series category manager. GREEN saves, EXIT cancels and BLUE restores;
 legacy filter exclusions are converted to editable hidden categories on save.
 R50 adds a fourth YouTube tile to the native welcome screen. A receiver-native
 search view lists compact video rows with keyless paged results and playback
 through the bundled extractor. Pagination follows every available continuation;
 quality controls share the existing YouTube settings. No IPTV account is needed.
 R51 covers the full receiver display with an opaque welcome screen while the
 artwork loads and stretches the welcome bitmap to the exact screen size.
 The YouTube results screen also keeps its video guard visible. The old unused
 welcome bitmap was removed and the current artwork recompressed losslessly.
 R52 pauses the underlying live DVB channel while the welcome screen is open
 and restores it on exit if no other service has started. Recordings and IPTV
 playback are left alone, so video cannot leak through side margins.
 R53 completes device and web translation coverage across 43 receiver catalogs
 and 47 web language choices. It fixes play-on-TV, media, subtitles, settings,
 status cards, pairing prompts, accessibility labels and localized errors.
